## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to apply k-way merge algorithms to partially sorted data, a common scenario in data processing pipelines and external sorting. It emphasizes understanding how the number of runs (r) impacts both time complexity and memory usage, connecting theoretical concepts with practical implementation.

## Key Concepts

- **k-way merge**: Efficient merging of multiple sorted sequences
- **Run detection**: Identifying sorted segments in a single linear pass
- **Min-heap**: Priority queue for selecting minimum elements efficiently
- **Complexity analysis**: O(n log r) time, O(r) auxiliary space
- **Edge cases**: Empty input, duplicate elements, and single-run scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor() { 
    this.heap = []; 
  }

  size() { 
    return this.heap.length; 
  }

  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this._heapifyUp(this.heap.length - 1);
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return null;
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const last = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = last;
      this._heapifyDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }

  _heapifyUp(index) { /* implementation */ }
  _heapifyDown(index) { /* implementation */ }
}

function kWayMerge(arr) {
  // Edge case: empty input
  if (arr.length === 0) return [];

  // Detect runs
  const runs = [];
  let start = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    if (i === arr.length || arr[i-1] > arr[i]) {
      runs.push({start, end: i-1, index: start});
      start = i;
    }
  }

  // Edge case: r=1 (already sorted)
  if (runs.length === 1) return arr.slice();

  // Initialize heap with first elements
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  const result = [];

  runs.forEach((run, i) => {
    heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId: i});
  });

  // Merge process
  while (heap.size() > 0) {
    const {value, runId} = heap.pop();
    result.push(value);

    const run = runs[runId];
    if (run.index < run.end) {
      run.index++;
      heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId});
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

The question tests ability to derive a linear-time, in-place solution for a data integrity problem common in interview tasks; relies on cyclic sort to exploit value-domain mapping, and requires careful handling of duplicates and missing numbers.

## Key Concepts

- In-place cyclic sort
- Handling duplicates and missing using array as hashmap
- Time/space complexity analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
function findMissingAndDuplicate(arr){
  const n = arr.length;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    while(arr[i]>=1 && arr[i]<=n && arr[arr[i]-1] !== arr[i]){
      const target = arr[i]-1;
      [arr[i], arr[target]] = [arr[target], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  let missing = -1, dup = -1;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    if(arr[i] !== i+1){
      missing = i+1;
      dup = arr[i];
      break;
    }
  }
  return {missing, dup};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for multiple missing/duplicates?
- How would you verify correctness in edge cases, e.g., near-complete arrays?
- Could you design a purely arithmetic/XOR-based O(n) time, O(1) space variant and compare trade-offs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of partition DP with sliding window optimization and modular arithmetic, plus dual implementation styles.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partitions
- Sliding window to enforce per-session bound
- Prefix-sum style accumulation for O(n) time

## Code Example

```javascript
function bottomUpCount(a, B) {
  const n = a.length, MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  let left = 0, window = 0, acc = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    window += a[i-1];
    while (window > B) {
      window -= a[left];
      acc = (acc - dp[left] + MOD) % MOD;
      left++;
    }
    acc = (acc + dp[i-1]) % MOD;
    dp[i] = acc;
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for 64‑bit sums to avoid overflow in JS/TS?
- How does this pattern relate to monotone queue optimizations in similar counting problems?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question combines multiple data structure concepts: per-user sliding windows within a global top-K context. It integrates arrays (per-user queues), hash tables for aggregates, and heaps for top-K retrieval with lazy invalidation. The problem tests a candidate's ability to:
- Design composite data structures for streaming workloads
- Handle time-based expirations efficiently
- Reason about algorithmic complexity under constraints
- Manage edge cases in production systems

## Key Concepts

1. **Sliding window per user** — Each user maintains their own event history, allowing O(1) expiration of old events via queue front-draining.
2. **Lazy invalidation** — The heap never explicitly deletes stale entries; instead, we check and skip them during retrieval. This keeps `addEvent` at O(log n) without expensive heap repairs.
3. **Aggregate maps** — Fast O(1) lookup and update of per-user totals, avoiding full recalculation.
4. **Tie-breaking** — Using `(delta, user_id)` as a composite key ensures stable, deterministic ordering.

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingActiveUsers {
  constructor(windowSeconds) {
    this.W = windowSeconds;
    this.userWindows = new Map();
    this.userTotals = new Map();
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap();
  }

  addEvent(ts, userId, delta) {
    if (!this.userWindows.has(userId)) {
      this.userWindows.set(userId, []);
      this.userTotals.set(userId, 0);
    }
    this.userWindows.get(userId).push({ ts, delta });
    const newTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId) + delta;
    this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);
    this.maxHeap.push({ delta: newTotal, userId });
    this.expireIfNeeded(userId, ts);
  }

  expireIfNeeded(userId, now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    const events = this.userWindows.get(userId);
    while (events.length && events[0].ts < cutoff) {
      const expired = events.shift();
      this.userTotals.set(userId, this.userTotals.get(userId) - expired.delta);
    }
  }

  topKUsers(k, now) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      const top = this.maxHeap.pop();
      if (!top || !this.isValid(top, now)) break;
      results.push(top.userId);
      this.maxHeap.push(top);
    }
    return results;
  }

  isValid(entry, now) {
    const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(entry.userId);
    return currentTotal !== undefined && Math.abs(currentTotal - entry.delta) < 0.0001;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis

| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| `addEvent` | O(log n) | O(n) |
| `topKUsers` | O(K log n) | O(K) |
| Expiration | O(m) amortized per event, where m = events expired | — |

## Common Pitfalls

- **Forgetting to re-push extracted heap entries** — corrupts the heap state
- **Not handling negative totals** — users with negative deltas are valid
- **Skipping expiration check in `topKUsers`** — stale heap entries will produce wrong results
- **Memory leaks** — empty deques should be removed from the map
